A big head on a long neck would be like holding a watermelon on the end of a fishing rod. Sauropods kept their heads small and light. Their teeth were pegs or spoons, made for raking leaves, not chewing.

Without chewing, they could eat fast. The food went to an enormous gut, where it sat for a long time while [[@digestion-gut|microbes broke it down]]. This let them feed almost constantly, and get very big.

{{story:blue-whale}}

Flamingo chicks can't strain food at first. Instead, both parents feed them a bright red liquid called crop milk, made in their throats. It is rich in fat and [[protein|protein]], and also full of the red pigment.

Feeding a chick for months drains a parent's colour. Many flamingo parents look noticeably paler by the end of the breeding season. A bright pink flamingo is usually a well-fed, healthy one, and brighter birds tend to attract partners more easily.

{{story:tomatoes}}

Blowing up a big bag with a pump would take far too long. Instead, the inflator contains a small amount of solid chemical. When the electric signal reaches it, the chemical reacts in a flash, producing a lot of hot gas, mostly nitrogen, the same gas that makes up most of the air you breathe.

That gas pours through a filter into the bag. Within a fraction of a second, a bag the size of a beach ball is ready. You might see a puff of powder afterwards. That's usually just cornflour or talc used to stop the folded bag sticking to itself.

{{story:road-safety}}

A copper wire is already packed with [[electron|electrons]], tiny charged particles that can move between [[atom|atoms]]. When the circuit closes, every electron along the wire starts to move at once, like marbles in a full tube. Push one in at one end and one pops out at the other almost instantly.

Each electron, though, only drifts a fraction of a millimetre per second. In homes in India and the USA, the current is alternating, so electrons mostly jiggle back and forth. In India they switch direction 100 times a second, and in the USA 120 times.

{{story:tesla-edison}}

Sundback worked on the problem for years. In 1913 he came up with the design we still use, with small cupped teeth instead of hooks. More teeth packed closer together made a fastener that held firmly but could still bend. He also designed a machine to stamp out the teeth and fix them to the tape, so it could be made cheaply.

In 1923, the B.F. Goodrich company used it on rubber boots and called them "Zippers", after the sound they made. The name stuck. Americans say zipper, while people in India and Britain usually say zip.

{{story:design-everyday}}

A Western violinist stands or sits on a chair with the violin under the chin. A Carnatic violinist sits cross-legged on the floor, with the body of the violin against the chest and the scroll, the curled top, resting on the right foot or ankle.

This position frees the left hand. Carnatic music is full of gamakas, ornaments where a note bends, slides or shakes. The player's fingers glide along the strings, sometimes several notes in one sweep. The strings are usually tuned to the main note, Sa, and to Pa, the fifth, in two [[octave|octaves]], instead of the Western tuning.

{{story:sound-travel}}

Some actions get lots of attention but make only a tiny difference to warming. Unplugging a phone charger saves very little. Swapping a plastic straw for a paper one is good for sea turtles, but it barely changes the climate.

That doesn't make them pointless. Plastic pollution is a serious problem in its own right. But if you want to cut warming, energy and food are the big [[lever|levers]]. Knowing the difference helps you spend effort where it counts.

{{story:carbon-cycle}}

Some of Escher's most famous prints are impossible. In Ascending and Descending (1960), monks march around a staircase that keeps going up, or down, forever. It's based on the "Penrose stairs", described by the father-and-son mathematicians Lionel and Roger Penrose in 1958.

The Penroses had seen Escher's work at a big maths conference in Amsterdam in 1954 and been inspired. They sent him their paper. Escher then used their impossible triangle to make Waterfall (1961). Ideas went back and forth like a game of catch. These drawings work as [[@optical-illusions|optical illusions]]: every small corner looks fine, and only the whole picture is impossible.

{{story:puzzle-impossible-shapes}}

In chaturanga, the foot soldiers crept forward slowly, just like pawns. The horse jumped in an odd bent path, because horses could leap over people in battle. That knight's L-shaped jump is one of the oldest rules in any game still played. It has barely changed in 15 centuries.

The chariot raced in straight lines, the way a chariot needs a clear road. It became the rook. The word comes from the Persian *rukh*, meaning chariot, which is why a rook looks nothing like a bird. The king, slow and precious, could move one square at a time. That has not changed either.

{{story:gupta-golden-age}}

1847 में उनके एक दोस्त, डॉक्टर याकोब कोलेत्श्का, की उँगली पोस्टमॉर्टम के दौरान एक छात्र के चाकू से कट गई। पोस्टमॉर्टम यानी लाश की जाँच। वे बीमार पड़े और चल बसे, और उनमें प्रसूति ज्वर जैसे ही लक्षण थे।

इसी से ज़ेमेलवाइस को सूझा। डॉक्टर लाशों की चीर-फाड़ करके बिना हाथ धोए सीधे माँओं की जाँच करने चले जाते थे। उन्हें लगा कि डॉक्टरों के हाथों पर “लाश के कण” लगे रह जाते हैं। दाइयाँ कभी पोस्टमॉर्टम नहीं करती थीं।

{{story:microscopes}}

रॉबर्ट वैडलो 1918 में आम आकार के पैदा हुए थे। 8 साल की उम्र तक वे अपने पिता से लंबे हो गए। 13 साल तक 2.2 मीटर पार कर गए। उनका शरीर ज़रूरत से ज़्यादा ग्रोथ हॉर्मोन बनाता था, और उन दिनों डॉक्टरों के पास इसे रोकने का कोई तरीका नहीं था।

वे अमेरिकी नाप के 37 नंबर के जूते पहनते थे, जो लगभग 47 सेमी लंबे थे, यानी एक आम आदमी के पैर से लगभग दुगने। चलने के लिए उन्हें टाँगों पर सहारे वाले पट्टे बाँधने पड़ते थे। एक पट्टे की रगड़ से हुआ छाला संक्रमित हो गया, और 1940 में 22 साल की उम्र में उनकी मौत हो गई। तब भी वे बढ़ ही रहे थे। ऑल्टन में उनके अंतिम संस्कार में 40,000 से ज़्यादा लोग पहुँचे।

{{story:growing-taller}}

Contagious yawning seems to work best between people who are close. In studies, people caught yawns more often from family and friends than from strangers. Very young children rarely catch yawns, and most only start around the age of four or five, when they also get better at understanding other people's [[@emotions|feelings]].

That has led some scientists to link catching yawns with empathy, the ability to feel what someone else feels. Others point out that the link is weak, and that some people who are very caring hardly ever catch yawns. It may be more about attention: you have to notice the yawn to catch it. Even so, people can catch a yawn just from [[@ears-hear|hearing]] one. Many catch it from words on a page, the way you might be doing now.

{{story:dogs-domestic}}

Look closely at a screen pixel and it is not really one dot. Each pixel has three even smaller parts, called subpixels: one red, one green and one blue. By making each part brighter or dimmer, the screen can mix almost any colour.

All three at full brightness look white. All three off look black. Red with green looks yellow. This works because your [[@eyes-see|eyes]] have three kinds of colour-sensing cells, tuned roughly to red, green and blue. A screen is a trick designed to fit your eyes.

{{story:eyes-see}}

Satellite internet is most useful where cables are hard to lay: mountain villages, deserts, islands, oil rigs, ships and aircraft. In the United States, many rural families who had no fast connection now use it. It has also been used after earthquakes and [[@hurricanes|hurricanes]], when towers on the ground were knocked out.

In 2025, India granted licences for satellite internet companies, including Starlink, to offer services, alongside Indian partners. ISRO and private Indian companies are also building [[@satellites-india|communication satellites]] of their own.

{{story:space-junk}}

The smell of a curry leaf is locked inside tiny pockets of oil in the leaf. Hold one up to a window and you can sometimes see them as pale dots. Chopping, crushing or heating bursts the pockets.

Most of those scented chemicals [[dissolve|dissolve]] better in fat than in water. So when a cook drops leaves into sizzling oil, the scent moves into the oil within seconds. Stirred through a dish, that oil carries the smell to every bite. Since much of what we call taste is really [[@taste-smell-link|smell]], this small step changes the whole meal.

{{story:taste-smell-link}}
